d certain di- and tripeptides, and is involved in innate immune response (PubMed:39719710). Preferably binds to short peptides with a basic residue at the first position and a hydrophobic residue at the second position (PubMed:39719710). Also able to transport carnosine (PubMed:31073693, PubMed:31254495). Involved in the detection of microbial pathogens by toll-like receptors (TLRs) and NOD-like receptors (NLRs), probably by mediating transport of bacterial peptidoglycans across the endolysosomal membrane: catalyzes the transport of certain bacterial peptidoglycans, such as muramyl dipeptide (MDP), the NOD2 ligand (By similarity)."}],"uniprot_source":"UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot Function, release 2026_04","retrieved":"2026-10-21"}],"structures":[],"alphafold_accessions":[]}